%erg 0.1
Title: erg check inside the archive reports every archived ticket as misfiled
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T12:02Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0285 bounded the v1 closure path test to the store: `StorePath()` tests
only the components below the directory `erg` was given. That fixed a store
failing wholesale because of a `*-closed` directory somewhere above it, and it
made every spelling of one store agree.

It also introduced this, measured pre- and post-0285 on built binaries:

```
$ cd tickets/closed && erg check
ERG CHECK FAILED (1 error):
  VIOLATION 0001-archived.erg: closed ticket not in closed/ directory -- run 'erg close ID' or 'erg archive' to file it
```

`erg check` with no argument auto-discovers the store (`findTicketsDir`), and
`looksLikeTicketStore` accepts any directory holding `.erg` files -- so from
inside the archive, the archive IS the store. Its tickets are then top-level
tickets carrying a `Closed:` header, which is exactly what the
closed-not-in-closed rule forbids. On `main` before 0285 this passed, because
the absolute path still carried the `closed` component.

Two things make it worth its own ticket rather than a footnote:

- **It needs no unusual argument.** 0285's log first called it "a degenerate
  invocation"; that was wrong. A bare `erg check` from a directory a user has
  every reason to `cd` into reaches it.
- **The remedy is self-defeating.** It tells the user to run `erg close` or
  `erg archive` on a ticket that is already closed and already archived -- the
  same class of message PR #348 fixed elsewhere.

## Why 0285 did not fix it

The information `StorePath()` has cannot tell `tickets/closed` (a store's
archive, addressed directly) from `dossier-closed` (a store that merely lives
in a directory whose name trips the rule). Both are "the store root's basename
reads as closed".

0285 tried the other branch first -- keep the root's own basename in the tested
path -- and it was worse: `erg check /path/dossier-closed` still failed
wholesale, and two relative spellings of that store disagreed, because
`filepath.Base(".")` is `"."` and carries no name. Excluding the root is the
better of the two available answers, and this ticket is its cost.

Note that the root's name cannot simply be allowed to *excuse* rather than
*accuse*, either: suppressing the closed-not-in-closed rule under a
closed-reading root also suppresses the open-ticket-in-closed rule, which is a
genuine violation that `main` catches inside `tickets/closed`.

## Candidate fix

Anchor the store root on a marker rather than on the argument. `.ergrc` already
lives at the store root and `loadConfig` (`src/go/config.go:21`) already reads
it, so `erg` could walk up from the given directory to the nearest `.ergrc` and
treat THAT as the root -- making `tickets/closed` resolve to `tickets/`, where
the archived ticket is correctly placed and an open one is correctly flagged.

That is a change to what every command considers the store, not a local repair,
which is why it is not folded into 0285. It also needs a decision for stores
with no `.ergrc`, since the file is optional: falling back to today's behaviour
is the obvious answer but should be stated, not assumed.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/erg.go` (`StorePath`) -- its doc comment states the cost this ticket owns
- `src/go/main.go` (`findTicketsDir`, `looksLikeTicketStore`, `resolveDir`) -- where the store root is decided
- `src/go/config.go` (`loadConfig`) -- already reads `.ergrc` from the store root
- `src/go/check.go` (`folderClosure`) -- the three placement rules and their remedies

## Test

Red step, in `src/go/erg_test.go` next to the 0285 guards: build
`tickets/closed/0001-archived.erg` with a `Closed:` header, resolve the store
from inside that directory the way `cmdCheck` does, and assert `folderClosure`
returns no violation. It must fail today -- run it and watch it report the
closed-not-in-closed violation before touching any code.

Control, in the same fixture: an OPEN header-less ticket in `tickets/closed/`
must still be flagged. A fix that silences the first case by suppressing the
rules under a closed-reading root silences this one too, and only the pair
catches that.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `cd tickets/closed && erg check` passes on a well-formed archive
- [ ] An open ticket misfiled into `tickets/closed/` is still reported, whether the store is addressed from inside or from above
- [ ] Every spelling of one store still yields one verdict (0285's `TestStoreVerdictIsIndependentOfSpelling` stays green)
- [ ] The behaviour with no `.ergrc` present is decided and stated in the spec
- [ ] No placement message recommends an action the user has already taken
- [ ] `make check` passes
